[
    {
        "content": "<p>Sometimes I want the <code>gcongr</code> tactic, but I do not want to import Mathlib. In such cases, I register patterns like the following in order to make <code>grind</code> do something similar to <code>gcongr</code>.</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"n\">grind_pattern</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Nat.mul_le_mul_right</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=&gt;</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">n</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">*</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">k</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">m</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">*</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">k</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">_</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">≤</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">_</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">where</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">k</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=/=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">0</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">k</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=/=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">1</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">guard</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">n</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">≤</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">m</span>\n\n<span class=\"kd\">example</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">a</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">b</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">c</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Nat</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">h</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">a</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">≤</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">b</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">a</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">*</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">c</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">≤</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">b</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">*</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">c</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kd\">by</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">grind</span>\n\n<span class=\"bp\">#</span><span class=\"n\">grind_lint</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">inspect</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Nat.mul_le_mul_right</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>I wonder why patterns like this are not registered by default.</p>",
        "id": 608316210,
        "sender_full_name": "Asei Inoue",
        "timestamp": 1783278920
    },
    {
        "content": "<p><code>grind</code> may not be well suited to handling this kind of problem, but I think this is a useful pattern. If it has few negative effects, I think it may be worth adding.</p>",
        "id": 608316390,
        "sender_full_name": "Asei Inoue",
        "timestamp": 1783279043
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>I think getting <code>grind</code> to do non-linear inequalities (such as your example) is still not a solved problem, but it was being worked on.</p>",
        "id": 608450943,
        "sender_full_name": "Jovan Gerbscheid",
        "timestamp": 1783345546
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Anyways, the ideal is to have specific inequality solver(s) in <code>grind</code> that solve such problems, rather that a set of patterns that sometimes work and sometimes don't.</p>",
        "id": 608451536,
        "sender_full_name": "Jovan Gerbscheid",
        "timestamp": 1783345681
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>I didn’t know there were efforts underway to make grind handle theorems like the one in my example as well. I see. That is something to look forward to.</p>",
        "id": 608451616,
        "sender_full_name": "Asei Inoue",
        "timestamp": 1783345701
    },
